# Wiring: `synthorg.meta.mcp.handlers.{{.Domain}}`

Generated by `synthorg new tool {{.Domain}}`. The handler module is
conventions-clean (typed args via `PaginationFields`, `parse_typed`
boundary call, `common_logging` helpers, structured success log via
`MCP_HANDLER_INVOKE_SUCCESS`). The steps below register the new tool
with the MCP domain dispatcher; applying them is a one-time manual
edit per scaffold.

## 1. Register the handler

Add the tool to a domain module under
`src/synthorg/meta/mcp/domains/`. The domain dispatcher reads
``ToolHandler`` instances from a ``MappingProxyType`` keyed by tool
name; pick the existing domain that matches your subsystem or create
``src/synthorg/meta/mcp/domains/{{.Domain}}.py``:

```python
from types import MappingProxyType

from synthorg.meta.mcp.handler_protocol import ToolHandler
from synthorg.meta.mcp.handlers.{{.Domain}} import _list as _{{.Domain}}_list

HANDLERS: MappingProxyType[str, ToolHandler] = MappingProxyType({
    "synthorg_{{.Domain}}_list": _{{.Domain}}_list,
})
```

Then expose the domain from the central registry (typically
``src/synthorg/meta/mcp/domains/__init__.py`` or the equivalent
aggregator).

## 2. Wire the consuming service

The handler reads ``app_state.{{.Domain}}_service.list_items``. Make
sure the consuming service is started at boot (see the
``synthorg new service {{.Domain}}`` scaffold's WIRING.md, or the
existing pattern under ``src/synthorg/api/lifecycle_helpers.py``).
Without that step the handler raises ``AttributeError`` on first
invocation.

## 3. Add destructive variants when needed

For admin ops (delete / mutate / admin), extend the args model
with ``AdminGuardrailFields`` from
``synthorg.meta.mcp.domains._common_args`` AND call
``require_admin_guardrails(arguments, actor)`` in the handler
body. The ``log_handler_guardrail_violated`` helper from
``common_logging`` covers the third structured log path.
